torch.expm1 on a dense sweep of |x| below the current thresholds at each dtype and confirm it stays inside atol + rtol*|reference|.", "expected_impact": "Removes roughly half the per-element vector operations (abs, compare, 3-op series, and the extf/truncf chain around them, plus one wide select) and leaves a single transcendental against a constant. Because the small cases are already pinned at the launch floor and only the three 4096x4096 cases carry the deficit, this can move geo_mean only through them: if the compare/series/conversion terms are a large share of vector time, expect the three big cases to move materially (fp16 0.46x/bf16 0.275x/fp32 0.285x trending toward 0.55-0.70x) and geo_mean toward or past 0.70. If vector time barely moves, `tl.exp` dominates, which is itself the decisive answer and redirects effort to overlap/pipelining.", "risks_and_rollback": "Accuracy risk concentrated at small |x|: relative error grows without bound as |x| shrinks, and only the absolute tolerance term absorbs it, so the full 18-case correctness suite must pass with matched_ratio 1.0 and a dense small-|x| sweep must be checked before adoption. Second risk is the sign of zero: expm1(-0.0) is -0.0 while exp(-0.0)-1.0 is +0.0; a compare-free version loses that sign, so if a correctness case or review flags negative zero, reinstate exactly one compare against zero plus a select (x==0 -> x) and re-measure - that still keeps the series and abs deleted. Third, fp16/bf16 overflow boundary shifts by one ulp (exp(x) can overflow where expm1 does not); this band is already present in the current code for all |x| >= TINY, so it is not a new exposure, but confirm the special-value cases still pass. Rollback is a single-file revert to main.py sha256 3a7da241fbbbdf27981cdd098c9030ed9cbc4ccc3de19710a05254e47a4482f0; do not retune tiles in the same round, so the result stays attributable.", "validation_workloads": ["expm1_-correctness-0", "expm1_-correctness-2", "expm1_-correctness-4", "e