Actually, let me add one more high-value thing now to save a job: the "no mask" variant at grid=40 (valid since 16777216 divisible by 40*8192? 40*8192 = 327680; 16777216/327680 = 51.2 — NOT divisible. So a mask-free loop needs a tail. Skip.)

But note: with GRIDC=40, step=40*8192=327680 and 16.7M/327680 = 51.2 iterations → each program does 51 full tiles + partial. The mask is still needed.

Hmm, what about choosing grid so that n/(grid*BLOCK) is an integer? For n = 16.7M = 2^24: grid must divide 2^24/BLOCK = 2048 for BLOCK=8192 → grid ∈ {32, 64, 128, 2048}. With 40 vector cores, grid=32 or 64. Then a mask-free main loop needs no tail at all, and each program does exactly 2048/grid iterations.

That's a nice idea: for 16.7M fp16, grid=64 with BLOCK=8192 → 32 iterations each, fully mask-free, and all offsets aligned. The last-tile mask only needed when not divisible (other sizes). So: a mask-free kernel variant used when n % (grid*BLOCK) == 0, else the masked loop.

